the way to interpret this is not so much  as a huge increase in interest rates  this is part of a return to normality  normal interest rates in normal times  are are positive with respect to the  inflation rate they're above the level  of inflation if we think that the  inflation rate is going to be two  percent we would typically expect  interest rates to be three percent or  more we've had some very unusual times  in the last decade which has meant that  interest rates after the financial  crisis have hovered around zero that is  been  unprecedented in in in monetary history  so we should actually be reasonably  pleased that interest rates are going to  return to something like normality 1.75  is a start we'd expect a few more hikes  as well but given an inflation rate at  least temporarily in double figures  this is not in any world a set of tight  monetary conditions interest rates are  still low and have to go higher the  level is the thing that really matters  not the change and in a sense the npc is  catching up here and doing a reasonable  job in doing so yeah indeed because many  other central banks around the world are  raised ahead of our central bank being  the bank of england of course and what  does it say to you about how the  governor andrew bailey and the other  members of the monetary policy committee  all of whom of course get a chance to  vote every month what does it say to you  about how they are feeling  about the economy and the economic  prospects in the uk going forward well  again their focus will always be on  delivering price stability defined by  the government as two percent subject to  not injecting too much output volatility  which is another way of saying having a  deep recession where unemployment rises  we'd prefer a more gradual increase of  inflation back to target rather than  something that was very rapid and led to  lots of people losing their jobs which  we don't want to see in an economy we  want people to be able to work uh and  earn a living so i i think the npc would  have taken a step back and said look  where are rates now 1.25 where do we  think inflation is going to be two years  ahead and where do we think interests  have to get to and i suspect  collectively they've taken the view it  has to get to around three percent um  over the course of this year or early  next year and they have to think  carefully about how to  move in the appropriate steps to get  there personally i would favor a more  gradual increase in interest rates but  what really matters is that is the final  level they reach and i think something  around three percent is the right level  and the reason i prefer a bit more  gradualism is that we don't exactly  understand how the economy will respond  it's a heavily indebted economy public  debt is about the size of gdp uh  household debt is also similarly about  the size of gdp and therefore these  interest rate rises may have more of an  impact than we might think and it might  take might make sense to move more  slowly than quickly but what matters is  getting three percent today with uh some  more breaking news coming through from  the bank of england um the uk they say  will enter five consecutive quarters of  recession with gross domestic product  falling by as much as 2.1 what do you  make of that well that's very much in  line with what we were discussing a few  moments ago the increase in oil prices  the increase in food prices  and the overall disruption to uh the  supply chains the uk is a  global and open economy is particularly  affected by these things as is our  tourist and recreational sector and we  ourselves didn't see much opportunity  for growth over the next year and a half  and i think the bank has followed a very  similar narrative this is a contraction  in activity that is sustained across  many sectors that means we're going to  be materially poorer in a year than we  might have anticipated some time ago  and what is this going to do for  sentiment i mean you've got a  double-edged sword here haven't you  you've got a prediction from the bank of  england of five consecutive quarters of  recession as soon as you say that word  everybody starts to panic and you've got  inflation rising i mean it's it's not  it's not appealing in any way is it no  no i mean stagflation has always been uh  portrayed as the central bankers  nightmare scenario and this is certainly  the most testing period the independent  central bank with his monetary policy  committees had to face since the  financial crisis and arguably including  that as well we had developed some tools  during the financial crisis to deal with  that that avoided a deep and prolonged  recession but now we have as you say  both inflation rising and output falling  but we can understand well why output is  falling for the reasons i've given a  large amount of the  goods and services that we have to buy  in from abroad have gone up in price um  and it's also the case that our trade  has compressed as a result of brexit and  and tourism is not back to the levels  that it that it was  uh pre-covered all of that means the  economy is going to contract and be uh  not as productive or not as large as it  as it might have been anticipated some  years ago  that's in a sense going to take care of  itself what the bank has to focus on is  is bringing that inflation rate down in  a gradual manner so it doesn't  exacerbate the downturn in the economy  the downturn as we've said is a result  of things that are happening mostly in  the rest of the world what the bank has  then to do is just  you know squeeze some of the demand out  of the economy so inflation returns  gradually to target
 

